\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Incidence and the exterior derivative}
\date{2026-11-03}


\p{A [vertex-field module](fdip-0010) is the potential #{\varphi}, and an
edge is a direction made discrete. The [ring-valued incidence
map](fdip-0010) is #{d} on #{0}-forms. Along an edge it is
#{a\cdot\nabla\varphi}, and on the edges #{x\to x+\mu} of a cubic
lattice it is the lattice gradient #{\varphi(x+\mu)-\varphi(x)}. A
[field pullback](fdip-0010) is composition with a map of the carriers,
as in the substitution #{f'(x)=f(h(x))}.}

\p{This answers those two questions. What remains of the gradient
is the difference of the values at the two ends of each edge. The
values may lie in any commutative ring, because that difference uses
only subtraction. The arithmetic part of these notes runs the same
differences, and Gaussian elimination, over finite fields
([Gaussian elimination at every prime](fdip-008M)). A
multivector-valued field needs a noncommutative coefficient algebra.
With commutative coefficients, each scalar component, and the scalar
#{a\cdot\nabla\varphi}, still matches the incidence map.
Hestenes obtains the inner product as the scalar part of the geometric
product in
\citet{author-PDF pp. 5-6, Equations (1.1)-(1.6)}{hestenes1986unified}.
Doran and Lasenby treat the vector derivative in
\citet{Geometric Algebra for Physicists}{doran2003geometric}.}
